(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-oxygen-delivery-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Oxygen Delivery Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Once oxygen has been started, what follows the clinical and objective assessment?","choices":["Stopping monitoring once saturation rises","Discharge home on the same device that day","Doubling the flow to secure the gain","Vital signs and monitoring for complications"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. After the clinical and objective assessment, the next steps are vital signs and further monitoring for complications. Oxygen carries hazards, so monitoring continues even after the saturation improves. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which statement fits the rationale that a nebulizer allows medication choice for specific treatment?","choices":["The liquid can be chosen for the condition","Oxygen is the sole thing nebulized","The dose is set by the oxygen flow","The device works without medication"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A nebulizer allows medication choice for specific treatment, meaning the liquid can be chosen to suit the condition being treated. It delivers liquid medication directly to the airways. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which interface is used to deliver air-entrainment nebulizer oxygen to a patient with a tracheostomy?","choices":["Simple mask","Trach collar","Nasal cannula","Non-rebreather mask"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A trach collar is the interface for delivering air-entrainment nebulizer oxygen to a patient with a tracheostomy. The air-entrainment nebulizer also uses the aerosol mask and open face mask over the face. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How commonly is the partial rebreather mask used in a clinical setting?","choices":["It is the first-line mask for mild hypoxia","It is the standard home oxygen device","It is the usual mask in severe hypoxia","Not as commonly as other masks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The partial rebreather mask is not as commonly used in a clinical setting as other oxygen masks. Its distinguishing feature is that some exhaled gas gets into the reservoir bag. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which headache type is a special indication for oxygen therapy?","choices":["Medication-overuse headache","Post-lumbar puncture headache","Cluster headache","Tension-type headache"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The stem asks which headache type is a special indication for oxygen therapy, and cluster headache is named there, alongside air hunger. These two are the special indications listed after hypoxia, acute needs and respiratory conditions. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which rule about smoking applies in the home of a patient using oxygen?","choices":["Visitors may smoke near an open window","Smoking is allowed in a different room","No one should smoke in the home","Smoking is allowed with the tank off"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. No one should smoke in the home of a patient using oxygen, and a No Smoking sign is posted to reinforce this. Oxygen is highly flammable, so the rule covers the entire home. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"For which degree of hypoxia is the nasal cannula most commonly used?","choices":["Mild hypoxia","Severe hypoxia with good ventilation","Mild to moderate respiratory distress","Hypoxia needing a precise fraction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The stem asks about the degree of hypoxia. The nasal cannula is a low flow device most commonly used in mild hypoxia, with an output of 24% to 44% at 1 to 6 L/min of flow. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which two airways are listed as artificial airways, as opposed to tracheal airways?","choices":["Nasal cannula and simple mask","Venturi mask and trach collar","Oropharyngeal and nasopharyngeal","Endotracheal tube and tracheostomy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The oropharyngeal airway and the nasopharyngeal airway are the two listed as artificial airways. The tracheal airways, the endotracheal tube and tracheostomy, are listed separately. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which hematocrit supports Group II home oxygen qualification?","choices":["Greater than 36%","Greater than 46%","Greater than 56%","Greater than 66%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A hematocrit above 56%, which indicates polycythemia, is one of the findings that supports Group II home oxygen qualification when the oxygen value is borderline (56 to 59 mm Hg or 89% saturation). — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A simple mask at 7 to 8 L/min delivers about what fraction of inspired oxygen?","choices":["35%","40%","50%","80%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. On a simple mask, 7 to 8 L/min delivers about 50% fraction of inspired oxygen. The scale is 5 to 6 L/min for 35%, 6 to 7 L/min for 40%, and 7 to 8 L/min for 50%, the top step. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What sleep finding qualifies a patient for nocturnal oxygen?","choices":["Any snoring witnessed by a bed partner","Saturation 92% with a 2% drop from baseline","Daytime sleepiness with a normal saturation","Saturation 88% or lower with a drop of 5% or more from baseline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. For nocturnal oxygen, the oxygen saturation must be 88% or lower during sleep, with a drop of 5% or more from the baseline. Both parts are needed to meet the sleep criterion. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Below what oxygen saturation is oxygen given to a patient with a myocardial infarction?","choices":["85%","91%","95%","98%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In a patient with a myocardial infarction, oxygen is not given unless the oxygen saturation is below 91%. That cutoff is the threshold named for this caution, tied to the risk of vasoconstriction. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Besides being flammable, which property of oxygen tanks shapes the teaching about storage and disposal?","choices":["They release carbon monoxide","They contain compressed gas","They contain a liquid fuel","They expire after one week"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen tanks contain compressed gas, in addition to oxygen being highly flammable, and that is why storage and disposal are taught. Tanks are stored and disposed of properly, not handled casually. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What does a nebulizer deliver?","choices":["A fixed fraction of oxygen","Powdered medication from a canister","Medication into the bloodstream","Liquid medication directly to the airways"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A nebulizer delivers liquid medication directly to the airways. That direct delivery is the first rationale listed for nebulizer therapy, ahead of secretion clearance and others. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which feature distinguishes a partial rebreather mask from a non-rebreather mask?","choices":["It has no reservoir bag","It delivers a fixed precise fraction","It is the most common hospital mask","Some exhaled gas enters its reservoir bag"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In a partial rebreather mask, some exhaled gas gets into the reservoir bag, which is what distinguishes it from a non-rebreather mask. The non-rebreather's one-way valve, in contrast, prevents inhaling exhaled air. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"In which situation is a nebulizer chosen over an inhaler?","choices":["When the patient prefers a pill","When the patient needs 100% oxygen","When the patient has mild stable symptoms","When an inhaler is hard for the patient to use"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A nebulizer is chosen over an inhaler when inhalers are challenging in certain situations, such as when the patient finds one hard to use. It is also used in severe exacerbations. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which blood gas change is listed as a hazard of oxygen therapy?","choices":["Hypercapnia from carbon dioxide retention","Hypocapnia from overbreathing","Methemoglobin build-up","Hypoxia worsening on oxygen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypercapnia from carbon dioxide retention is listed among the hazards of oxygen therapy, alongside fire, mucosal irritation and vasoconstriction. It is the only blood gas change in the list of hazards. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which of these is listed as a benefit of oxygen therapy?","choices":["Mucosal irritation","Carbon dioxide retention","Vasoconstriction","Symptom relief"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The stem asks for a benefit of oxygen therapy, and symptom relief is listed as one, with correction of hypoxia and therapeutic benefits. The other three choices are on the list of risks. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"For which situation is a simple oxygen mask used?","choices":["Mild to moderate respiratory distress","Mild hypoxia needing a few liters","Severe hypoxia with good ventilation","Hypoxia needing a fixed fraction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The stem names a situation of respiratory distress. A simple oxygen mask is a low flow device set at 5 to 10 L/min and used in mild to moderate respiratory distress, delivering about 35% to 50%. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Through a nasal cannula, each 1 L/min increase in flow raises the fraction of inspired oxygen by about how much?","choices":["2%","4%","10%","21%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Each 1 L/min increase in nasal cannula flow raises the fraction of inspired oxygen by about 4%. For example, 1 L/min gives 24%, 2 L/min gives 28%, and 3 L/min gives 32%, up to 44% at 6 L/min. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the usual flow range of a nasal cannula?","choices":["1 to 6 L/min","4 to 10 L/min","5 to 10 L/min","10 to 15 L/min"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A nasal cannula is set between 1 and 6 L/min, and across that range its fraction of inspired oxygen runs from 24% to 44%. Humidity is used when the flow is above 4 L/min, so only the top settings. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How often should a home oxygen user test the smoke and carbon monoxide alarms?","choices":["Weekly","Monthly","Every 6 months","Every year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Working smoke and carbon monoxide alarms are part of home oxygen safety, and they should be tested monthly. Oxygen is highly flammable, so alarms are a backup against fire. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the target oxygen saturation when evaluating response to oxygen therapy?","choices":["A fixed 88% for every patient","A fixed 95% for every patient","It varies from patient to patient","A fixed 100% for every patient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When the response to oxygen is evaluated, the saturation should improve, but the target varies from patient to patient. No single saturation number applies to every patient. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Besides delivering medication, which airway problem is a rationale for nebulizer therapy?","choices":["Correcting a fixed airway narrowing","Replacing an endotracheal tube","Reducing carbon dioxide retention","Secretion clearance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Secretion clearance is a listed rationale for nebulizer therapy, in addition to delivering liquid medication directly to the airways. The other rationales are choice of medication, inhaler difficulty and severe exacerbations. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"When is the testing for home oxygen qualification performed?","choices":["At a scheduled annual physical","At the time of need","After a year of symptoms","At the first diagnosis of lung disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Testing for home oxygen qualification is performed at the time of need, with documentation of hypoxia by arterial blood gas or pulse oximetry during rest, exertion and/or sleep. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"On an arterial blood gas, which change shows a good response to oxygen therapy?","choices":["Lower arterial oxygen with a lower pH","Unchanged oxygen with a higher pH","Higher arterial oxygen with no rise in carbon dioxide","Higher arterial oxygen with a rise in carbon dioxide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A good response on an arterial blood gas is an increase in the arterial oxygen pressure without an increase in the carbon dioxide pressure, together with correction of the pH. Oxygen should help without causing retention. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the flow range of a simple oxygen mask?","choices":["1 to 6 L/min","5 to 10 L/min","10 to 15 L/min","15 to 25 L/min"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A simple oxygen mask is set between 5 and 10 L/min and is a low flow device used in mild to moderate respiratory distress. At those settings it delivers about 35% to 50% fraction of inspired oxygen. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which three conditions documented in a patient make a borderline oxygen value qualify for home oxygen?","choices":["Pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale, polycythemia","Asthma, allergic rhinitis, sinusitis","Hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia","Anemia, obesity, sleep apnea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A moderately abnormal value qualifies for home oxygen when it comes with evidence of a chronic disease state or complication: pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale or polycythemia. These patients are recertified at 3 months. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Above what fraction of inspired oxygen is an air-entrainment nebulizer not recommended?","choices":["0.30","0.50","0.80","1.0"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An air-entrainment nebulizer can deliver higher fractions of inspired oxygen, but use above 0.50 is not recommended. The recommended ceiling is 0.50, which corresponds to 50%. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"To qualify for home oxygen, hypoxia must be documented by which methods?","choices":["Arterial blood gas or pulse oximetry","Chest radiograph or spirometry","Complete blood count or venous gas","Patient report or a symptom diary"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Home oxygen requires documentation of hypoxia by arterial blood gas or pulse oximetry, measured during rest, exertion and/or sleep. The testing is done at the time of need. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"}]};
